mission of an ineligible target and on the observed outcome, not on the future's wording alone.\n - The control case shows the same mechanism transfers leadership correctly to a voter, so the defect is specific to the eligibility of an explicitly named target, not to leadership transfer in general.\n - The measured disruption is small in absolute terms because the cluster ran with inmemConfig timeouts (Heartbeat/Election/Lease 50 ms): 8 samples and about 80 ms without a leader, one extra election round. The interval follows the configured election timeout, so a production configuration of about 1 s would scale it accordingly; the 80 ms figure is not a bound for other settings.\n - No committed entry was lost, no vote was counted for an ineligible candidate, and no decision-safety property was contradicted in either case; what is contradicted is the completion signal and the authority outcome of the explicit transfer.\nLIMITS:\n - Both runs exercised one cluster size (four members, three voters after the demotion) and one relationship: a leader transferring to a non-leader member. Transfer initiated while the leader later regains leadership, and clusters whose only non-voter is the sole reachable member, were not tested.\n - Leaderless counts are sampled every ~10 ms over roughly 4.7 s of the 5 s window (470 samples); a gap shorter than the sample spacing would be missed, so 80 ms is a lower bound for the trigger and the control's zero does not prove uninterrupted leadership.\n - The demoted target's own configuration still showed it as a voter at transfer time in both runs. The exercised case is therefore a member that believes it is eligible while the voters' committed view refuses it; a target that already knows it is a non-voter was not exercised, although timeoutNow's unconditional candidacy implies it would campaign as well.\n - That the voter members had committed the demotion before the transfer is inferred from their rejection and term adoption, not from rea